    {
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "13170243-ac23-422f-a99f-7eca2d1d0a32",
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "## `destandardize_channels`\n\n```python\ndef destandardize_channels(batch, channel_order, scalers):\n    x = batch[\"ts\"].clone()\n    for i, channel in enumerate(channel_order):\n        x[:, i, ...] = scalers[channel].inverse_transform(x[:, i, ...])\n    return {**batch, \"ts\": x}\n```\n\nIt takes a batch whose `ts` tensor (shape `B, C, T, H, W`) is in **normalized** space \u2014 the zero-mean/unit-variance form the dataset/model normally consume \u2014 and, channel by channel, applies that channel's scaler's `inverse_transform()`. That call undoes only the z-score (subtracting the mean and dividing by std, in reverse), so the result lands in **signum-log space**, not raw physical units. It returns a new batch dict (original untouched) with `ts` replaced by that de-standardized tensor.\n\n`IdentificationModel` (right below it) expects its input already in signum-log space \u2014 that's why this preprocessing step exists: to convert the dataset's normalized output back to signum-log before it's fed to the linear baseline.\n\n## What \"signum-log space\" is\n\nThe data pipeline normalizes each channel in two stages:\n\n1. **Signum-log compression** \u2014 `sign(x) * log1p(|x|)`, applied to raw physical values (DN for AIA channels, Gauss for HMI magnetic components, etc.). This compresses the huge dynamic range of solar imaging data (values spanning many orders of magnitude) into something more tractable, while preserving sign.\n2. **Z-score** \u2014 per-channel standardization (subtract mean, divide by std) of the signum-log values, giving the normalized tensor the model actually sees.\n\nSo there are three spaces in the pipeline (documented in `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py:137-167`):\n\n- **normalized** \u2192 what the model/dataset output \u2014 zero-mean, unit-variance per channel\n- **signum-log** \u2192 z-score undone, but still `sign(x)\u00b7log1p(|x|)` \u2014 this is what `destandardize_channels` produces\n- **physical** \u2192 both stages undone, real DN/Gauss/m-per-s units \u2014 what you'd want for plotting or a physically-meaningful loss, via `HelioNetCDFDataset.inverse_transform_data()`\n\nThe key gotcha the code comments emphasize: `scaler.inverse_transform()` is *not* the full inverse of the dataset's `transform()` \u2014 it only reverses the z-score, landing you in signum-log, not physical units. Conflating the two is called out as the easy way to silently feed a model wrong numbers.",
      "time": 1789498950.7990844,
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "body": "What is a channel?",
      "metadata": {
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "settings": {},
        "model": {
          "id": "sonnet",
          "settings": {}
        }
      },
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "0a7c7a6c-13b9-4b42-b914-ca3d1415659e",
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "smildred",
      "time": 1789498974.0981216
    },
    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "time": 1789498982.2011123,
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "A **channel** here is one of the physical measurement types stacked together as input to the model \u2014 analogous to RGB channels in a normal image, but instead of 3 color channels, Surya uses 13 channels of solar observations from SDO (Solar Dynamics Observatory):\n\n- **8 AIA channels** \u2014 extreme-ultraviolet/UV images at different wavelengths (e.g. 94, 131, 171, 193, 211, 304, 335, 1600 \u00c5), each sensitive to plasma at a different temperature, giving different views of the solar atmosphere.\n- **5 HMI channels** \u2014 magnetic field components (magnetogram data) measuring the Sun's magnetic field.\n\nSo a full input sample has shape `(C=13, T, H, W)` \u2014 13 stacked 2D images (each `H\u00d7W`, e.g. 4096\u00d74096) at each timestep, all co-registered to the same spatial grid.\n\nEach channel has its own physical units and dynamic range (DN counts for AIA, Gauss for HMI), which is exactly why normalization is done **per-channel**: `channel_order` is the list of channel names in the order they sit in that `C` dimension, and `scalers` maps each channel name to its own fitted scaler \u2014 because a scaler tuned for one wavelength's DN range wouldn't make sense applied to a magnetogram's Gauss range.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "7114af55-179c-497e-bcf1-d8c44431bc10"
    },
    {
      "metadata": {
        "settings": {},
        "model": {
          "settings": {},
          "id": "sonnet"
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
      },
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "903ccde1-16ca-4e5b-852a-38d2ac099196",
      "sender": "smildred",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "What all do you suppose I should change in models, metrics and LightningModule to make the model move from a flare classification model to solar event identification.",
      "time": 1789499323.374042
