      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Confirmed the bug. In `train_metrics` and `val_metrics` (lines 120, 151), raw logits from the linear head are passed straight into `BinaryAccuracy`:\n\n```python\noutput_metrics[\"BinaryAccuracy\"] = self.BinaryAccuracy(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1))\n```\n\ntorchmetrics' `BinaryAccuracy` only applies `sigmoid()` if the incoming values fall **outside** `[0, 1]` (verified in the installed `torchmetrics==1.8.2` source \u2014 `_binary_stat_scores_format` calls `normalize_logits_if_needed`, which is a no-op when all values already look like they're in `[0,1]`). Since your linear head is untrained/lightly-trained, its logits cluster near 0 and can easily land inside `[0, 1]` by chance \u2014 in that case they're treated as *already-computed probabilities* and thresholded directly at 0.5, instead of being passed through sigmoid first. That's exactly the failure mode from your earlier output: `BinaryAccuracy = 0.0` while BCE loss looked normal \u2014 the accuracy computation silently skipped the sigmoid.\n\n**Fix:** apply `.sigmoid()` explicitly before passing to `BinaryAccuracy`, in both `train_metrics` (line 120) and `val_metrics` (line 151):\n\n```python\noutput_metrics[\"BinaryAccuracy\"] = self.BinaryAccuracy(preds.reshape(-1).sigmoid(), target.reshape(-1))\n```\n\nThis makes the accuracy computation correct regardless of where the logits happen to sit, rather than depending on torchmetrics' fragile auto-detection heuristic.\n\nTwo smaller, non-bug items while I'm in the file:\n- Module docstring (lines 1-16) still says `FlareMetrics` / MSE / RRSE \u2014 leftover from before the rename, now inconsistent with the actual `SolarEventMetrics` class and BCE/accuracy metrics.\n- `__call__`'s docstring (line 169) still references `FlareLightningModule`.\n\nWant me to apply the sigmoid fix and the docstring cleanup?",
      "type": "msg"
    },
