      "id": "66f5df56-40c6-4cec-83d9-9b8b874be301",
      "body": "Good \u2014 the sigmoid fix is in place (I can see it in the file at lines 120/151), so this `0.0` is no longer a metric-computation artifact \u2014 it means the model really got every sample in this call wrong. A few likely explanations, roughly in order of likelihood:\n\n1. **This is from an untrained/early model.** If this is Lightning's sanity-check pass or the first few steps, the linear head is still close to its random init and hasn't learned anything yet \u2014 a small batch landing at exactly 0% by chance isn't alarming on its own. What step/epoch is this from, and what's the batch size? If it's a batch of 2-4, a single unlucky flip flips the whole accuracy number.\n\n2. **Class imbalance + a model that's collapsed to one class.** If `Solar_Event_Occurrence` is mostly 0s (or mostly 1s) in the data, and the linear head's bias term has pushed all predictions to the majority-opposite side, you'd see accuracy near 0% consistently rather than by chance. Worth checking the label distribution in `ds_flare_index` / `solar_Dataset` \u2014 `python -c \"import pandas as pd; print(pd.read_csv('<path>')['Solar_Event_Occurrence'].value_counts())\"`.\n\n3. **The pooling might be destroying the signal.** `IdentificationModel.forward` does `x.abs().mean(dim=[3,4])` \u2014 collapsing the entire spatial map to a per-channel scalar via mean. If the event signature is spatially localized (e.g. a small active region), averaging over the full 4096\u00d74096 frame could wash it out entirely, leaving the linear layer with near-uninformative features \u2014 in which case it settles into always predicting one class, and depending on which batch you looked at, that can show up as exactly 0%.\n\nIs this consistently 0% across many batches/steps, or did you just check the very first one? And do you know the label balance in your index?"
    }
